{
  "answer": "Paragraph 10 and footnote 1 support continuing ultimate responsibility despite outsourcing; they do not supply a complete test for fulfillment. A justified conclusion would require an applicable standard and evidence distinguishing retained authority and accountability, actual supervisory conduct, and the soundness of operations and ownership records. Contractual allocation, a supervision entry or a completed reconciliation alone does not establish fulfillment. No actual Product Provider facts or sufficient discharge standard are supplied.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "inference": "The text prevents outsourcing from displacing the Product Provider’s ultimate responsibility. As a proposed evidential approach, contracts and governance arrangements should show who can obtain information, direct corrective action and hold providers accountable. Those particular mechanisms are not an express statutory checklist in the quoted passage.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "Applicability of the retained edition to the entity, product, activity and assessment period, including relevant conditions or relaxations.",
        "Evidence that the assessed entity is the Product Provider.",
        "Actual outsourcing contracts, governance arrangements and effective decision-making powers.",
        "An applicable standard determining when retained authority and accountability satisfy the requirement."
      ],
      "proposition": "Allocation of authority and accountability is relevant to retained responsibility, but contractual allocation alone does not establish fulfillment.",
      "status": "Text-supported responsibility; proposed evidential deduction",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u38.3432"
        },
        {
          "quote": "the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Actual monitoring, review, escalation and remediation can help establish whether retained responsibility was exercised. The quoted paragraph does not define their required frequency or adequacy. UT 6.11C supplies express supervision language for outsourced valuation, but transferring that standard to tokenisation generally requires a separate justification.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "An applicable supervisory standard, including required scope, frequency, competence and response to deficiencies.",
        "Evidence of what supervisors examined, found and did throughout the relevant period.",
        "If relying directly on UT 6.11C, proof of its applicability, the management-company role and outsourced valuation activity."
      ],
      "proposition": "Supervisory conduct must be assessed substantively; recording a supervision event does not by itself establish adequate supervision.",
      "status": "Deduction; adequacy unresolved",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "For factors of consideration, please also refer to Part A of the Appendix to the Tokenised Securities Circular, where applicable, when formulating the tokenisation arrangement.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The valuation activities of such third party should be subject to ongoing supervision and periodic review by the management company.",
          "unit_id": "ut-current.parent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "The requirement concerns substantive operational soundness and ownership record keeping. Paragraph 11 reinforces the need for proper records and operational compatibility as a separate requirement. Reconciliation is a relevant consideration, but completion alone does not establish accurate reflection of legally valid interests or resolution of discrepancies.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The applicable meaning and assessment criteria for operational soundness and proper ownership records.",
        "Evidence of investors’ legal interests, authoritative records, settlement finality and service-provider compatibility.",
        "Reconciliation scope, frequency, results, exceptions and corrective action.",
        "Evidence covering the relevant period rather than an isolated successful event."
      ],
      "proposition": "Operational and record-keeping outcomes require evidence beyond performed procedures; a completed reconciliation is not a sufficient discharge test.",
      "status": "Text-supported objects; evidential sufficiency deduction",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should ensure that proper records of token holders’ ownership interests in the product are maintained and the tokenisation arrangement is operationally compatible with service providers involved2.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u41.3664"
        },
        {
          "quote": "whether the ownership interest of the underlying assets of the Tokenised Securities are legally valid under the applicable governing laws, whether there are regular reconciliations between the records for the extrinsic rights and the records for the distributed ledger tokens, or whether any encumbrance affects the extrinsic rights.",
          "unit_id": "tokenised-securities.appendix"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Separately evaluating allocation, conduct and outcomes is a defensible assessment proposal, not a source-established necessary-and-sufficient test. Neither good outcomes alone nor formal retention alone proves fulfillment. The supplied text also does not establish that every operational failure necessarily constitutes breach irrespective of the governing standard.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "An authoritative interpretation resolving how allocation, conduct and outcomes determine fulfillment.",
        "The regulatory effect of the source’s SHOULD wording in the applicable authorisation context.",
        "Actual facts and evidence of deficiencies, responses and continuing performance."
      ],
      "proposition": "Applicability of continuing responsibility does not establish fulfillment or conclusively determine the legal effect of an operational failure.",
      "status": "Interpretive judgment; fulfillment not established",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u38.3432"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "legal_correctness_established": false,
  "question_id": "p10",
  "rival_readings": [
    "Allocation reading: paragraph 10 principally identifies who remains ultimately accountable despite delegation; substantive performance standards come from related requirements.",
    "Conduct reading: retained responsibility entails effective management and supervision; the passage leaves the adequate-conduct threshold unspecified.",
    "Outcome reading: responsibility for operational soundness and ownership record keeping requires substantive adequacy; the passage does not settle whether this imposes an absolute outcome obligation.",
    "Combined reading: assess allocation, supervisory conduct and outcomes together. This is a plausible synthesis, but the supplied sources do not establish it as a complete discharge test."
  ],
  "unresolved": [
    "Which interpretation and sufficiency standard govern the particular Product Provider and assessment interval.",
    "Whether actual authority, supervision, operations and records satisfy that standard.",
    "How referenced UT provisions and Appendix factors apply without conflating distinct actors and activities.",
    "Formal rule applicability, graph acceptance and serialization checks do not establish source-to-rule validity or legal fulfillment."
  ]
}
